<h2>Installing an Artifact as an App — How to Use Install as app, and Its Three Permission Levels</h2>

<figure class="doc-img">
    <img src="assets/ai/claude/tip/tip-install-as-app/hero.webp" width="1440" height="756"
         alt="Two overlapping windows drawn in rough black lines on an apricot background, with a mouse pointer in the middle of the white front window">
    <figcaption>Lead image of the Install as app announcement (image: Anthropic)</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>Ask Claude to "make me a meeting prep page" and a small web screen appears next to the chat. A runnable result like this is called an <strong>artifact</strong>. Until now an artifact only opened inside Claude. Even for a tool you used every day, you had to open Claude and find that conversation each time.</p>

<p><strong>Install as app</strong>, released in beta on November 24, 2026, lets you install an artifact as an app on Mac and Windows. An installed app gets its own icon and its own window. With your permission it can send notifications, sit in the menu bar, or work with your files. This page walks through it in the order you would use it: building, installing, setting permissions, and the settings an organization admin controls.</p>

