<p>An installed app is not a copy of the artifact. It shows the same artifact in another window, so when you or another editor changes the artifact, the app changes too. It calls Claude and uses your connectors (outside services such as mail and calendar that you have linked to Claude) the same way the artifact does.</p>

<h3>1. Build — describe the app you want</h3>
<p>Write what you want in a chat. The Help Center gives examples like these.</p>
<pre><code>Make a Pomodoro timer that lives in my menu bar.
Make a small Mac app that reminds me to stretch every hour.
Make a Windows app that renames the photos in a folder by date.</code></pre>
<p>The three examples need different permissions. The timer uses a menu bar item and notifications, the stretch reminder uses notifications, and the photo renamer touches your files, so it needs the highest level, full access. The section "Three permission levels" below covers what each one means.</p>
<p>Anthropic also showed apps its own staff built with the feature: a layered image editor, a file manager styled like a movie prop, an app that lets Claude tidy your files by folder rules, a terminal with Claude explaining alongside, and a menu bar app for watching your computer's performance. For everyday work, the announcement mentions a meeting prep page that regularly pulls from calendar and email, and a tracker for the orders that need to ship that day.</p>

<h3>2. Install</h3>
<ol>
    <li>Open the artifact in the main Claude Desktop window.</li>
    <li>Open the artifact's title menu, or right-click the artifact.</li>
    <li>Select <strong>Install as app</strong>. The same item is under Share.</li>
    <li>Select <strong>Install</strong> to confirm.</li>
</ol>
<p>You can also ask Claude to put an install button inside the artifact. Whichever path you take, Claude asks you to confirm before installing.</p>
<p>If you don't see Install as app, update Claude Desktop first. You need version 6.28899.0 or later. On macOS go to <code>Claude &gt; Check for Updates</code>; on Windows it is <code>Help &gt; Check for Updates</code> under the menu button (☰). On Team and Enterprise plans, ask an owner whether the feature is turned on.</p>
<p>"Add to Dock," "Add browser shortcut" and "Add to Home Screen" in your browser are something else. They only add a shortcut, without the notifications or file access described here.</p>

<h3>3. Open and quit</h3>
<table>
    <thead><tr><th>Where</th><th>How to open</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>macOS</strong></td><td>The <strong>Claude Apps</strong> folder inside the Applications folder in your home folder. Apps also show up in Spotlight and the Apps launcher, and <code>Options &gt; Keep in Dock</code> on the Dock icon pins one</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Windows</strong></td><td>The <strong>Claude Apps</strong> folder in the Start menu, or the desktop shortcut</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Claude Desktop</strong></td><td>"Open installed app" on the artifact, or <code>File &gt; Apps</code>, select the app, then "Open"</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
<p>An app with a menu bar item or system tray icon <strong>keeps running after you close its window</strong>. To stop it on macOS, quit it from the Dock or press Cmd+Q while it is the active app. On Windows, select "Exit" in the tray icon's menu.</p>

<h3>Three permission levels — what you allow decides what the app is</h3>
<p>This is the part to read most carefully. Installing an app does not let it touch your computer. What it can do grows only as far as you allow.</p>

