<p>An app has to ask before it uses any of the features below, and you answer each request with "Allow" or "Don't allow."</p>
<table>
    <thead><tr><th>Permission</th><th>What the app can do</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Notifications</strong></td><td>Show notifications while you use other apps</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Keyboard shortcuts</strong></td><td>Respond to key combinations while you use other apps</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Float over other windows</strong></td><td>Draw content above other windows, and read where the mouse cursor is on your screens</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Menu bar item (macOS) or system tray icon (Windows)</strong></td><td>Show an item there while the app is open</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Full access to this computer</strong></td><td>Run programs and read, change or delete your files, without asking each time. Also covers the permissions above</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<p>A few rules come with these answers.</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>An "Allow" covers one version of the app.</strong> After any update Claude asks again, including after your own edits. A "Don't allow" stays until you change it.</li>
    <li><strong>Code loaded from elsewhere can change without a new request.</strong> If an app loads code from another site, that part can change without Claude asking you again. This matters most for an app you gave full access.</li>
    <li><strong>Unclear origin brings a warning.</strong> If an app comes from outside your organization, or Claude can't tell who made it, the permission request shows a warning.</li>
    <li><strong>Full access is open only to authors you can trust.</strong> An app partly written by someone outside your organization, or one whose author Claude can't confirm, generally can't even request full access. On individual plans (Free, Pro, Max, Ultra), "outside your organization" means every user other than you. In practice an individual user can give full access only to apps they made.</li>
</ul>
<p>Anthropic doesn't review apps before you install them. An app with full access can run any program and delete your files, so allow it only if you trust everyone who wrote the app.</p>
<p>Sorting actions into tiers by how risky they are, and keeping the riskiest tier closed to most callers, is the same design this wiki looked at in <a href="#!news-20261022-claude-duet">Claude Duet at a Glance</a>, with its three-tier approval gate. Duet draws the tiers around an agent's actions. Install as app draws them around an app's permissions.</p>

<h3>Changing permissions and removing an app</h3>
<p>You can take a permission back at any time.</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>macOS</strong>: with the app open, click the app's name in the menu bar and select "Permissions."</li>
    <li><strong>Windows</strong>: right-click the app's title bar, or press Alt+Space, and select "Permissions."</li>
    <li><strong>Claude Desktop</strong>: while the app is open, go to <code>File &gt; Apps</code>, select the app, and select "Permissions."</li>
</ul>
<p>Turning off full access can reload or close the app, so save your work first. The programs the app started stop, except any that separated themselves from the app. Changes the app already made to your files are not undone.</p>
<p>To remove an app, go to <code>File &gt; Apps</code>, select it, and select "Uninstall." On macOS the app moves to the Trash; on Windows its shortcuts are removed. Uninstalling also removes the permissions you allowed, but <strong>the artifact itself stays</strong>.</p>

<h3>Plans and organization settings</h3>
<p>The beta is rolling out on every plan. The defaults differ by plan.</p>
<table>
    <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>Install apps</th><th>App permissions</th><th>Full access</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Free, Pro, Max, Ultra</strong></td><td>Available</td><td>Available</td><td>Only for apps you made</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Team</strong></td><td>On by default</td><td>On by default</td><td>Off by default (an admin turns it on)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Enterprise</strong></td><td>Off by default</td><td>Off by default</td><td>Off by default</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Customer-managed encryption keys (CMEK), organization-wide ZDR</strong></td><td colspan="3">Start with all three off</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>HIPAA-ready configurations, hybrid deployments</strong></td><td colspan="3">Can't be turned on</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>K-12 organizations</strong></td><td colspan="2">Some start with these off</td><td>Can't be turned on</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
<p>ZDR stands for zero data retention, a contract term under which the service keeps no record of your conversations. Admins should know that <strong>installed apps are not covered by ZDR</strong>.</p>
<p>Team and Enterprise admins control three separate settings in <code>Organization settings &gt; Artifacts</code>: Install as app, App permissions, and Full access to the computer. A change takes up to about an hour to apply. Turning a setting off closes open apps and stops the programs they started. Installed apps and the permissions people gave stay in place and work again if the setting is turned back on.</p>
<p>To cover personal accounts on company computers too, use device policy (MDM, the system a company uses to manage settings on employee devices remotely). There are three keys, and they can only turn features off. Where organization settings and device policy differ, the stricter one applies.</p>
<pre><code>isArtifactAppsEnabled             # false turns off installed apps entirely
isArtifactAppPermissionsEnabled   # false turns off app permissions and full access
isArtifactAppFullAccessEnabled    # false turns off full access only

# macOS: a computer-level profile in the com.anthropic.claudefordesktop domain
# Windows: DWORD 0 under HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Claude</code></pre>
<p>The audit trail has gaps. Opening an app and access checks are logged in the Compliance API as artifact views, but <strong>installs, permission answers, what an app does on the computer, and changes to the three settings are not logged</strong>. Weigh that before opening full access to an organization.</p>

<h3>Know before you use it</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Mac and Windows only.</strong> Apps aren't available on Linux, web or mobile.</li>
    <li><strong>No offline use.</strong> You need to be online and signed in to Claude Desktop.</li>
    <li><strong>It uses your limits.</strong> Building an app, and any request an app makes to Claude, counts toward your plan's usage limits. Several apps that call Claude on a schedule will use up that much more.</li>
    <li><strong>Some artifacts can't be installed.</strong> Artifacts made from a template such as Docs, Slides or Sheets, legacy artifacts, and live artifacts from Claude Cowork are excluded.</li>
    <li><strong>Losing access stops the app.</strong> If the owner stops sharing it with you, the programs it started stop too. This can take up to about 20 minutes, and longer while Claude is offline or signed out.</li>
    <li><strong>Computer use only half fits.</strong> On macOS, Claude can see an installed app when it uses your computer, but can't click or type in it.</li>
</ul>

<h3>If you're starting out</h3>
<ol>
    <li>Pick one artifact you already open every day and install it. Use it for a few days at the default level, with no permissions given.</li>
    <li>When you want notifications, allow notifications and nothing else. App permissions are asked one feature at a time, so open only what you need.</li>
    <li>Give full access only to an app you built yourself, and only when working with files is what the app is for. Before you do, ask Claude whether the app loads code from an outside site.</li>
</ol>

